#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc12d2e-e51b-5a42-96cd-b305f65fb37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290342} {:answer/id #uuid "db1c7f3a-6ecc-55c5-809a-0863430e9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290343} {:answer/id #uuid "98c45658-526f-5c3d-a7a4-904eaec5c7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290344} {:answer/id #uuid "eb56b96c-6b1e-5509-9c5c-9596df9d1218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290345} {:answer/id #uuid "9123b0fe-37e0-53a2-b9b6-a4027f596f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc12d2e-e51b-5a42-96cd-b305f65fb37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290342} :answer-field/id #uuid "062b6761-8a83-56a7-ab24-c797cc9067ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76f508a1-f65b-5d1d-8bbf-94be0b492cbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104064" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{4}-{2}^{3}-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-C} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}{x}^{3}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbaab87-e17f-594a-82f3-26b498f6ff7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (\\frac{e^{4x} + 4e^{5} - 1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099035} {:answer/id #uuid "9f35677b-1906-5ffe-b2dd-3f9faf09f08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (e^{4x} - \\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099036} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0b0be7-2032-5e96-aad5-e8860ea31814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (\\frac{e^{4x}}{4} + C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099037} {:answer/id #uuid "5a66d415-5075-5f0c-9895-7054368c0962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (e^{4x} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099038} {:answer/id #uuid "67d7f28a-ec4c-50ff-90bb-d36f6a06710f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (\\frac{e^{4x}}{4} + e^{5} + \\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cbaab87-e17f-594a-82f3-26b498f6ff7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\ln (\\frac{e^{4x} + 4e^{5} - 1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099035} :answer-field/id #uuid "99b1a4f0-9160-59d8-b07d-ffb852645886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e844aafc-6198-5e85-8b96-36f86924e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-20071" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{4x - y}$ that satisfies $y(0) = 5$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Multiply by $e^y$ and integrate:\n$$e^y\\frac{dy}{dx}=e^{4x},\\qquad e^y=\\frac14e^{4x}+C.$$\nAt $x=0$, $y=5$, so $C=e^5-1/4$. Therefore\n$$y=\\ln\\left(\\frac{e^{4x}+4e^5-1}{4}\\right).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8eb70a-e41b-5bb2-8a1c-c2d892adb8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292424} {:answer/id #uuid "782f563b-2517-5b65-b3d1-0639cd3da9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a8eb70a-e41b-5bb2-8a1c-c2d892adb8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292424} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d39f3ea-9301-57f8-ab08-d613b890416e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2221490e-f21a-588c-b91a-30cee47bc521" :question/math-academy-id "q-347853" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}=0$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =2x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2(0)+C} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{4x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f9f1a5-f46a-52b5-808c-b763c4421c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290376} {:answer/id #uuid "01cde3e0-f862-5b29-80fd-e43104620586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290377} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f769f3-c488-5e60-8b10-e07de00a892f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290378} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc5f637-d6a4-59de-b6d2-103abdf33cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290379} {:answer/id #uuid "952d6638-c733-51a4-82c1-b8f542a4dbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f769f3-c488-5e60-8b10-e07de00a892f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290378} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd55144-c2ba-57a4-82ec-bee36392a6bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65fc80a1-7721-5e98-9055-6f369b27046a" :question/math-academy-id "q-8309" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$"}]}
